d.rep(r"""The phase projection is a retention of a supplied block, the whole population, and the dynamics selects nothing, so clauses (F2), (F5), and (F6) of \cref{inh:recursive-formation} are not addressed. The retention is non-injective, and an admitted child-level probe, uniform child sampling, distinguishes configurations in one fiber; but that probe is not inherited by the parent, so (F3) fails for it, and if the probe class is reduced to responses computed from the phase law, (F4) fails instead. The null-flow control of \cref{inh:null-flow} shows where the reciprocal interaction enters. With every peer cost deleted, each agent follows the diagonal field \cref{eq:public-phase-diagonal} independently, the linearized difference and common modes then have the same spectrum $\mathcal A$, and the rate condition \cref{eq:public-phase-gap} fails, since the difference rate $1-|J|$ is smaller than $L$. The spectral gap $\kappa_N$ that makes the phase a compression of differences is therefore produced by the peer costs, but no grouping is selected, and the results of this chapter are retention and identifiability statements rather than formation.""",
      r"""The objective is the Gibbs-reduced reciprocal potential with identity channels, and the dynamics is its chart flow on $\mathsf E^N$. The phase projection is a retention of a supplied block, the whole population, and the dynamics selects nothing, so clauses (F2), (F5), and (F6) of \cref{inh:recursive-formation} are not addressed. The child-level probe rule $B_I$ consists of the identity peer channels applied to each member, with response law that member's law, and of uniform child sampling. The retention is non-injective on the forward-invariant neighborhood, and a member's channel separates two configurations in one fiber that differ in that member's law, so the separation required by (F4) exists; it is transient, since every fiber converges to one point, and it is not of symmetry type, since the fibers are continuous families of heterogeneous configurations rather than orbits of a symmetry. For (F3), every declared parent protocol rule $A_I$ that contains uniform child sampling fails, because that response is not constant on the fibers of $\Pi$ and, by \cref{cor:public-phase-unique}, no Lipschitz intertwining single-law parent preserves it. A rule consisting of responses computed from the phase law is defined from the retention and is not a declared rule in the sense of \cref{inh:protocol-classes}. With the empty rule, (B) is vacuous and (L), (D), and (C) hold for $\Pi$.

The source sets exclude the receiver, so the two nulls of \cref{inh:null-flow} coincide. With every peer cost deleted, each agent follows the diagonal field \cref{eq:public-phase-diagonal} independently, the linearized difference and common modes have the same spectrum $\mathcal A$, and the rate condition \cref{eq:public-phase-gap} fails, since the difference rate $1-|J|$ is smaller than $L$. The null flow nevertheless admits exact intertwining retractions onto the diagonal. On the binary subfamily, the function $\mathfrak h(x)=x\exp\int_0^x[-(1-J)/f_b(s)-1/s]ds$ is analytic near zero, with $\mathfrak h'(0)=1$ and $\mathfrak h(\phi^b_tx)=e^{-(1-J)t}\mathfrak h(x)$, and for every $c\geq0$ the map $\Pi^0_c(x_1,x_2)=\mathfrak h^{-1}\bigl((\mathfrak h(x_1)+\mathfrak h(x_2))/2+c|\mathfrak h(x_1)-\mathfrak h(x_2)|\bigr)$ is a Lipschitz retraction onto the diagonal that intertwines the null flow with $\phi^b_t$, by homogeneity of degree one, and has one-dimensional fibers; $\Pi^0_0$ is analytic. These retractions are not unique in the Lipschitz class, and their fibers are not strong-stable leaves: two configurations in one fiber approach each other only at the common rate. What the peer costs produce is therefore the spectral gap $\kappa_N$, which makes the Lipschitz intertwining parent unique (\cref{cor:public-phase-unique}) and makes the discarded differences decay faster than the parent signal, not the existence of an exact compressing coordinate. No grouping is selected, and the results of this chapter are retention and identifiability statements rather than formation.""")
